 a slash or a dot that the path rule would catch. The docs describe `-m a.b` support without mentioning this limit.","fix_commit":"b89cc9efb5d501c0e262209cee68128392d51e21","id":"F002","line":176,"path":"agentic_preflight/risk.py","response_note":null,"severity":"low","stage":"review","status":"fixed","suggestion":"Also accept `-mNAME` and short-flag clusters that end in `m`, and add tests for both forms.","title":"Attached `-mmodule` and combined interpreter flags such as `-Bm module` are not recognized","unit":"U0012"},{"action":"auto_fix","code_owned":false,"detail":"`command_path_patterns` reads `-m`, any short-flag cluster ending in `m` (`_MODULE_FLAG`), and any word starting with `-m` plus a letter (`_ATTACHED_MODULE`) as a Python module flag, whatever program the command runs. When the next word, or the attached value, is not a dotted name (for example `reviewer -vm prompts/review.txt` or `reviewer -mprompts/review.txt`), `_module_patterns` returns [] and the loop `continue`s. That skips the ordinary path check, so `prompts/**` is not protected, even though the same word after any other flag would have been. This makes the parser protect less than its documented rule that a word with `/` or `.` counts as a path.","fix_commit":"105a83cc8d203b70eddc4d9e62f73ccac637f024","id":"F003","line":171,"path":"agentic_preflight/risk.py","response_note":null,"severity":"low","stage":"review","status":"fixed","suggestion":"When the module value fails `_MODULE_NAME`, fall through to the ordinary path handling instead of `continue`. Add test cases such as `reviewer -vm prompts/review.txt` -> [\"prompts/**\"].","title":"Words after any `-\u2026m` flag are read as module names, and a path there is dropped","unit":"U0012"}],"fingerprint":{"base_tree_sha":"725a2d01be4301c7430140c65460bee2beec119e","config_sha256":"25363ddc7d586ecf84e8f7f7fa372aa5c9e7aa56e8216e0ac04452b84d670e9c","configuration_supported":true,"diff_sha256":"c9c09d7f07cddb8ba412d132d5296098477845a3fec05fe0584e0f9